Phantom-WG Modern — Setup Guide
=================================

Prerequisites:
  - Linux with kernel 5.15 or newer (the kernel docker info reports)
  - Docker Engine 27.0.1 or newer, with the Docker Compose plugin 2.27.2 or
    newer. Engine 27.0.1 is the first with ip6tables on by default, which the
    dual-stack network of the stack needs; Compose 2.27.2 is the first with
    "docker compose config --environment", which the tools read .env with.
  - /dev/net/tun
  - Python 3.9 or newer as python3. Without python3, tools/prod.sh stops and,
    on the Debian, Fedora/RHEL and Arch families, names the command that
    installs it; on an older python3 it stops and names the version it found.
  - bash
  - git, to install from a clone and to update
  - unzip, to install from a release zip
  - A user of the docker group, or root, to run the tools. The environment
    files, the keys, the auth database, the self-signed certificate and the
    Cloudflare mode belong to the owner of the install directory; the address
    pool, the data of the running stack and what certbot writes belong to root

  ./tools/prod.sh gates reports what this host has: Python, Docker, Compose,
  kernel, TUN, git and free disk.

Directory Structure:
  phantom-wg/
  ├── Dockerfile                           Daemon image (multi-stage build)
  ├── nginx.Dockerfile                     Nginx image
  ├── docker-compose.yml                   Production stack (3 services)
  ├── docker-compose.override.yml.example  Compose override template
  ├── .env.example                         Image source and network subnets
  ├── .env.daemon.example                  Daemon environment template
  ├── .env.auth-service.example            Auth service environment template
  ├── .env.cf.example                      Cloudflare mode settings template
  ├── .gitignore                           Files of this machine, out of git
  ├── requirements.txt                     Python dependencies (pinned, hashed)
  ├── SETUP                                This file
  ├── UPDATE                               Update and maintenance guide
  ├── MIGRATION                            Migration from 1.1.5 to 2.0.0
  ├── CHANGELOG                            Changes of each release
  ├── LICENSE                              AGPL-3.0
  ├── THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES                 Dependency licenses
  ├── images/                              Image archive (release zip only)
  ├── phantom_daemon/                      Daemon source
  ├── services/
  │   ├── auth-service/                    Authentication service
  │   ├── nginx/                           TLS reverse proxy config
  │   └── react-spa/dist/                  Web dashboard (pre-built)
  └── tools/
      ├── prod.sh                          Production CLI, starts the toolchain
      ├── __main__.py                      Toolchain entry (Python floor check)
      ├── core/                            Command discovery and process runner
      ├── stack/prod/                      Production commands and libraries
      └── compose/                         Image sources and their manifest

Quick Start:
  1. Setup (first time only):
     ./tools/prod.sh setup

     This creates what is missing, in this order:
       - .env, .env.daemon, .env.auth-service and
         docker-compose.override.yml, each from its .example
       - WireGuard server keypair
       - Auth service keypair, admin account and database
       - Self-signed TLS certificate
       - Address pool (container-data/db/wallet.db)

     Setup Recipe below lists the commands it runs.

     The superadmin account is named admin. To name it otherwise:
       ./tools/prod.sh setup --admin-username <name>

     Setup refuses a complete install and otherwise creates only what is
     missing. It stops on what it cannot finish: a certificate without its
     key or the reverse, an auth database without its keys, a subnet for a
     pool that exists. To start over, use ./tools/prod.sh hard-reset.

     Credentials are written to:
       container-data/secrets/production/
       container-data/secrets/production/.admin_password

  2. Configure endpoints:
     Edit .env.daemon and set your server's public IP:
       WIREGUARD_ENDPOINT_V4=203.0.113.1
       WIREGUARD_ENDPOINT_V6=2001:db8::1    (optional)
     The stack starts without them: they go into the WireGuard client
     configs the dashboard exports. The daemon reads them when it starts, so
     set them before up.

  3. Start:
     ./tools/prod.sh up

  4. Access:
     Dashboard:  https://<server-ip>
     WireGuard:  UDP port 51820

Setup Recipe:
  The install as commands to run by hand, without the tool, from the install
  directory, as a user of the docker group. It is for a directory before
  setup: the tool checks what is in place and stops, these commands do not.

    # Host: Engine 27.0.1+ on kernel 5.15+, Compose 2.27.2+
    docker info --format '{{.ServerVersion}} {{.KernelVersion}}'
    docker compose version --short
    test -e /dev/net/tun

    # Local files
    for f in .env .env.daemon .env.auth-service docker-compose.override.yml; do
      [ -f "$f" ] || cp "$f.example" "$f"
    done

    # Images: PHANTOM_IMAGE_SOURCE in .env, one of
    SOURCE=registry
    docker compose pull                                # registry
    docker compose build                               # build
    cat images/VERSION                                 # archive: the version
    grep image: tools/compose/registry.yml             #   of the three pins
    (cd images && sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS)             # archive
    while read -r _ t; do
      docker load -q -i "images/$t"
    done < images/SHA256SUMS
    LEG="tools/compose/$SOURCE.yml"
    DAEMON=$(docker compose -f "$LEG" config --images daemon)
    AUTH=$(docker compose -f "$LEG" config --images auth-service)

    # Subnet. SUBNET is the value of --terazi-ipv4-subnet, or empty. A check
    # that refuses it prints its reason and leaves SUBNET empty: stop there,
    # the pool would take the default.
    SUBNET=
    [ -z "$SUBNET" ] || SUBNET=$(docker run --rm \
      -v "$PWD/phantom_daemon:/app/phantom_daemon:ro" \
      "$DAEMON" python -m phantom_daemon.base.wallet.subnet_check "$SUBNET")

    SECRETS=container-data/secrets/production
    mkdir -p "$SECRETS" container-data/auth-db container-data/db

    # WireGuard keypair
    GENERATE='from wireguard_go_bridge.keys import generate_private_key as new'
    GENERATE="$GENERATE, derive_public_key as public"
    GENERATE="$GENERATE; key = new(); print(key); print(public(key))"
    docker run --rm "$DAEMON" python -c "$GENERATE" | {
      read -r private; echo "$private" > "$SECRETS/wg_private_key"
      read -r public; echo "$public" > "$SECRETS/wg_public_key"
    }
    chmod 600 "$SECRETS/wg_private_key" "$SECRETS/wg_public_key"

    # Auth service: signing and verify keys, admin password, auth.db
    docker run --rm --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" \
      -v "$PWD/$SECRETS:/secrets" -v "$PWD/container-data/auth-db:/db" \
      -v "$PWD/services/auth-service/tools/bootstrap.py:/tmp/bootstrap.py:ro" \
      "$AUTH" python /tmp/bootstrap.py \
      --secrets-dir /secrets --db-dir /db --admin-username admin
    chmod 600 "$SECRETS/auth_signing_key" "$SECRETS/auth_verify_key" \
      "$SECRETS/.admin_password"

    # Self-signed TLS certificate
    SUBJECT='/C=TR/ST=Istanbul/L=Istanbul/O=Phantom-WG'
    SUBJECT="$SUBJECT/OU=Phantom Certification Authority"
    SUBJECT="$SUBJECT/CN=phantom-wg/emailAddress=ca@phantom.tc"
    docker run --rm --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" -v "$PWD/$SECRETS:/secrets" \
      "$DAEMON" openssl req -x509 -newkey ec \
      -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:prime256v1 -days 365 -nodes \
      -keyout /secrets/tls_key -out /secrets/tls_cert -subj "$SUBJECT" \
      -addext 'subjectAltName=DNS:phantom-wg,DNS:localhost,IP:127.0.0.1' \
      -addext 'basicConstraints=critical,CA:TRUE' \
      -addext 'keyUsage=critical,digitalSignature,keyCertSign,keyEncipherment' \
      -addext 'extendedKeyUsage=serverAuth'
    chmod 600 "$SECRETS/tls_cert" "$SECRETS/tls_key"

    # Address pool
    docker run --rm --network none \
      -v "$PWD/phantom_daemon:/app/phantom_daemon:ro" \
      --mount "type=bind,src=$PWD/container-data/db,dst=/db" \
      "$DAEMON" python -m phantom_daemon.base.wallet.bootstrap \
      /db ${SUBNET:+"$SUBNET"}

    # ./tools/prod.sh up
    docker compose up -d
    # All running, restart counts steady for 10 s:
    docker compose ps -aq | xargs docker inspect \
      --format '{{.Name}} {{.State.Status}} {{.RestartCount}}'
    docker compose ps

Custom Client Subnet:
  VPN clients get their addresses from 10.8.0.0/24 by default. Terazi pairs
  every IPv4 address with an IPv6 address by index, so this one value sets
  both pools. A /24 holds 253 clients. To start with another subnet, pass it
  to the first setup:

    ./tools/prod.sh setup --terazi-ipv4-subnet=10.9.0.0/24

  The value is used once: setup creates wallet.db and fills it with the pool,
  and from then on the subnet lives there. To move to
  another subnet, start over with ./tools/prod.sh hard-reset. The prefix length
  alone can be changed from the dashboard (/16 to /30); every WireGuard client
  config must then be exported again.

  Use a private subnet with a prefix between /16 and /30.

Images:
  The stack runs the release images from the registry by default.
  They are built by CI from the Dockerfiles in this directory.
  To build the same Dockerfiles on this machine instead, after setup:

    ./tools/prod.sh images use build
    ./tools/prod.sh up

  To stay off the registry from the first step, choose before setup:

    cp .env.example .env
    (set PHANTOM_IMAGE_SOURCE=build in .env)
    ./tools/prod.sh setup

  A release zip carries the images in images/ and starts on the archive
  source, so it installs without reaching the registry. Each of the three
  sources can be selected once .env is written; archive needs the images/ of
  a release zip:

    ./tools/prod.sh images use <source>
    ./tools/prod.sh images sync
    ./tools/prod.sh up

  The choice is PHANTOM_IMAGE_SOURCE in .env (registry, build or archive).

Environment Files:
  .env                      Image source and network subnets
  .env.daemon               Daemon configuration (WireGuard, storage)
  .env.auth-service         Auth service configuration (JWT, rate limiting)

  These files are created from .example templates during setup.
  They are NOT tracked by git — safe for user-specific values.
  Edit them to customize subnets, timeouts, or any service parameter.

  A change in .env.daemon or .env.auth-service reaches a running stack with
  ./tools/prod.sh up, which starts the service again; restart does not read
  the file.

Compose Override:
  docker-compose.override.yml is created from its .example during setup.
  It is NOT tracked by git and belongs to this machine. Compose applies it
  over docker-compose.yml: edit it to change anything specific to this server.

Commands:
  ./tools/prod.sh help             Show all commands
  ./tools/prod.sh gates            Report what this host provides
  ./tools/prod.sh setup            First setup (env, keys, auth, TLS, pool)
  ./tools/prod.sh gen-keys [-f]    Generate the WireGuard keypair
  ./tools/prod.sh setup-auth [-f]  Bootstrap the auth service
  ./tools/prod.sh setup-tls [-f]   Generate a self-signed TLS certificate
  ./tools/prod.sh add-superadmin <name>
                                   Add a superadmin account (running stack)
  ./tools/prod.sh up               Start stack (stops if setup is incomplete)
  ./tools/prod.sh down             Stop stack
  ./tools/prod.sh restart [svc]    Restart all services, or one
  ./tools/prod.sh images           Show the image source and its images
  ./tools/prod.sh images use <src> Select registry, build or archive
  ./tools/prod.sh images sync      Pull, build or load the selected images
  ./tools/prod.sh pull             Pull the registry images
  ./tools/prod.sh build            Build images (build source)
  ./tools/prod.sh rebuild          Rebuild from scratch (build source)
  ./tools/prod.sh update           Pull latest (git clone), recreate stack
  ./tools/prod.sh show-versions    Show daemon + bridge versions
  ./tools/prod.sh logs [service]   Follow logs (all, or one service)
  ./tools/prod.sh status           Container status
  ./tools/prod.sh shell [service]  Open shell (default: daemon)
  ./tools/prod.sh exec <svc> <cmd> Execute command in service
  ./tools/prod.sh certbot <domain> Obtain Let's Encrypt TLS certificate
  ./tools/prod.sh cloudflare [action]
                                   Serve through Cloudflare (status, on,
                                   refresh, off)
  ./tools/prod.sh hard-reset       Wipe ALL data (destructive)

  With -f, gen-keys, setup-auth and setup-tls regenerate what exists.
  gen-keys -f invalidates every WireGuard client config. setup-auth -f moves
  auth.db aside as auth.db.<time>.bak and creates a new admin account.
  setup and setup-auth take --admin-username <name> (default admin).

  add-superadmin adds a superadmin account through the running auth
  service. It asks the password twice on the terminal, without echo, and
  takes it as typed. The auth service checks the name (letters, digits, _
  and -, up to 64) and the password (8 to 256 characters with an upper
  case letter, a lower case letter, a digit and a special character), and
  writes the account with its user_created audit row (actor system:host).
  One line and the exit code tell the result:
    SUPERADMIN_CREATED=<name>   0
    INVALID_USERNAME            4
    WEAK_PASSWORD               5
    USER_ALREADY_EXISTS         7
  A name that starts with "-" comes after "--": add-superadmin -- -name.

Ports:
  443/tcp     HTTPS (nginx → auth-service → daemon)
  51820/udp   WireGuard

  The published ports are set in docker-compose.override.yml.

Logs:
  docker-compose.yml rotates the log of each container: json-file driver,
  three files of 10 MB at most. ./tools/prod.sh logs [service] follows them.

  A login refused before the user is known is written to the auth-service
  log, not to the audit log: a wrong name or password, or too many
  attempts. Each is one line with the client IP and the name sent, if
  any; the password is never written. A wrong TOTP or backup code comes
  after a correct password and stays in the audit log.
    ./tools/prod.sh logs auth-service

Data Directories (created at runtime):
  container-data/
  ├── secrets/production/         Keys and certificates
  ├── db/                         Address pool with its clients, multihop exits
  ├── state/db/                   Runtime state (bridges)
  ├── auth-db/                    Authentication database
  ├── certbot/                    Let's Encrypt state (certbot only)
  └── cloudflare/                 Cloudflare mode (hard-reset leaves it)

TLS Certificate:
  Default is self-signed. To use your own certificate,
  replace the files before starting the stack:
    container-data/secrets/production/tls_cert
    container-data/secrets/production/tls_key

  Let's Encrypt (optional):
    ./tools/prod.sh certbot <domain>
    ./tools/prod.sh restart nginx

  The same two commands renew it: certbot takes a new certificate only when
  the one in place is near its end. They can run from cron.

Cloudflare:
  The stack can sit behind Cloudflare's proxy. In the Cloudflare dashboard
  of the zone, set the SSL/TLS mode to Full (strict) and turn
  Authenticated Origin Pulls (Global) on; on stops on any other setting.
  Then:
    ./tools/prod.sh cloudflare on       (writes .env.cf from .env.cf.example)
    (fill CF_API_TOKEN and CF_ZONE in .env.cf)
    ./tools/prod.sh cloudflare on
    ./tools/prod.sh up

  .env.cf belongs to this machine and the tool holds it at 600. Its token
  is of this zone only, with Zone - Zone - Read, Zone - Zone Settings -
  Read and Zone - SSL and Certificates - Read and Edit. CF_HOSTNAMES lists
  the names of the certificate; empty, the zone and *.<zone>.

  on makes a key here and takes an Origin CA certificate for it, writes the
  nginx snippets and the firewall rules to container-data/cloudflare/, and
  adds the mode to docker-compose.override.yml: NET_ADMIN for nginx, the
  command that loads the rules, the snippets and the certificate. nginx
  then requires Cloudflare's origin pull certificate, its port 443 drops
  connections from addresses outside Cloudflare's list, and it logs the
  visitor's address from CF-Connecting-IP. The override changes only after
  Compose accepts the new one; the last one stays as
  docker-compose.override.yml.bak. A step of on that fails after the
  certificate revokes it again, until the mode is recorded; a stop after
  that leaves the mode on, and off takes it back.

    ./tools/prod.sh cloudflare           Show the mode
    ./tools/prod.sh cloudflare refresh   Take Cloudflare's address list and
                                         origin pull CA again, then:
                                         ./tools/prod.sh restart nginx
    ./tools/prod.sh cloudflare off       Revoke the certificate and take the
                                         mode back, then: ./tools/prod.sh up
    ./tools/prod.sh cloudflare off --keep-certificate
                                         Take the mode back without the token;
                                         the certificate stays in Cloudflare

  off puts the override back, removes container-data/cloudflare/, and nginx
  takes its certificate from container-data/secrets/production/ again.
  .env.cf stays for the next on. hard-reset leaves the mode in place.

Updating:
  See the UPDATE file for the full update and maintenance guide.

  Quick reference:
    ./tools/prod.sh update

  Keep server-specific changes in docker-compose.override.yml.